    "slot_value": "A person must verify anything that affects the astrophysical content, calibration assumptions, source classification, or the exact error-model application before output is produced. This also includes anything that changes which Gaia processing variant or downstream product the data is meant to support. Low-risk items such as formatting conveniences, file naming, ordering, or clearly implied defaults may proceed on their own only when the scientific interpretation stays the same; if there is any doubt about whether the meaning changes, it must be treated as needing verification. Cosmetic changes are only self-approvable if they are truly presentation-only and do not affect how another Gaia tool parses, matches, or validates the output; file renames are harmless only when the receiving process does not rely on naming conventions, field reordering is harmless only when the format is explicitly order-insensitive, and filling a missing default must be checked whenever it could change which stage variant, calibration path, or product class downstream users think they are getting.",
    "operation": "refine"
  },
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-7-2-2",
    "slot_key": "Checkpointing, restart, or recovery behavior",
    "slot_value": "When storage or export is the bottleneck, splitting into smaller batches is usually the safest move because it lets the system checkpoint progress and avoid one huge failure.",
